1. `if you wanted to keep only one letter from a continuous letter sequence
    of same letters` : for this use `s.erase(unique(s.begin(),s.end()),s.end())`
    like `AABBAA` this will give you `ABA` and not `AB` to get `AB` you need to
    first sort the string so all things get in a proper sequence.
    
    
2. `finding index of minimum number in a vector array`: first find the minimum 
    element using `min_element` and then index of that using `mini-d.begin()` 
    and store in a integer data type. And similarly we can use this concept to 
    find maximum .
     ```cpp
    auto mini = min_element(d.begin(),d.end());
    int mini_i = mini-d.begin(); 
    ```
    
    
3. `going up one level in subdirectory level in terminal` : use `cd ..`


4. `going down one leve in subdirectory level in terminal`: `cd ~/Desktop` this will 
    take you in desktop and then type `cd` + `valid_sub_directory` that subdirectory 
    must exist in current working directory.
    
    
5. `ls -F -a` : this will saw all files even hidden files means and even `./` that will
    be represent our current working directory and `../` will be previous directory(means
    parent directory of our working directory) then our current directory and even other 
    files present in working directory will be get listed by this command. and doing `cd .`
    won't do anything because that will change our current directory to current directory 
    means no change will happen but by `cd ..` this will change our current directory to its
    previous parent directory . 
    
    
6. `combining ` : we can combine multiple commands using `-` as `ls -F -a` could be written as 
    `ls -Fa`.


7. `going to home directory directly`: typing `cd` command without any argument this will reach us 
    to our home directory. 
    
    
8. `relative and absolute path`: using `ls` and `cd` we can define either absolute or relative path
    , but there is one more to define absolute path `~/` use as prefix here `~` home directory and `/` 
    root path and define the path you require and this will work as absolute path.
    
    
9. `~`: terminal detects this as current user's home directory.
	
	
10. `cd -`: this would world like jump button in tv's remote means you could directly jump to the previous 
    directory by using this command and again typing this you would back to current working directory . 
	
	
11. `General syntax of a terminal` : `$ ls -F /` this is the general syntax then `$` will be known as `prompt`
    second word will be `command` , third word will be `option` and fourth word will be `argument` and `command`
    ,`option` or `argument` could contain any possible number of letters(but that should be valid ). And `-` is 
    short option and `--` long option. `arguments` tells what to operate on command (target) wherease `option` 
    will control how command will behave.Sometimes options and arguments are referred to as **parameters**. A 
    command can be called with more than one option and more than one argument, but a command doesn’t always 
    require an argument or an option. sometimes `options` are even referred as `switches or flags` when takes 
    no argument.
